<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Yeke Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1856 CE – 1891 CE (approx.) · capital Bunkeya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Msiri's trading kingdom in Katanga, a crossroads of copper, ivory and slave routes, ended by a Congo Free State expedition in 1891.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri</a>, a Nyamwezi trader from western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> around 1856. By backing local factions against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kazembe">Kingdom of Kazembe</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire</a>, he made himself ruler, with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeke-people">Yeke</a> followers as the core of a new state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri's</a> kingdom, known as Garanganze, controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to both the Atlantic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coasts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders brought guns and cloth in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, copper and slaves. Msiri ruled through relatives, chiefs and warbands; the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-stanley-arnot">Frederick Arnot</a> settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a> in 1886.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In December 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri</a> was shot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omer-bodson">Captain Omer Bodson</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stairs-expedition-to-katanga">Stairs Expedition</a>, sent by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Free State</a>, and his successor Mukanda-Bantu became a subordinate chief.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yeke-kingdom.jpg" alt="Yeke Kingdom" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Rexparry sydney; Credit: Rex Parry · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Msiri%27s_kingdom_in_1880_760x460_lo-res.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1856 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bunkeya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Msiri, Mukanda-Bantu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kiyeke, Kiluba, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Garanganze, Garenganze, Msiri's kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=yeke-kingdom" aria-label="Yeke Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5423 2566 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yeke Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5743 2822L5362 2652"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5362 2652h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5743 2822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5743 2822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bunkeya. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=yeke-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
